s a chance the landlord could have a bomb. \n\nBut the landlord has 18 cards left, which is a lot. If I play the 2s, I might force them to use their bombs or rockets early. But since the landlord has a lot of cards, they might not have a bomb. The card count shows that there\'s only one 2 left, so maybe there\'s a bomb with 2s? No, a bomb is four of a kind. Since there\'s only one 2 left, the maximum is three 2s in the landlord\'s hand. So no bomb there. \n\nSo playing the 2s would be a strong move. It\'s the highest possible pair. Unless the landlord has a rocket (which is the highest), but rockets are only two jokers. The card count shows one BlackJoker left. Wait, the RedJoker is in my hand. So the BlackJoker is still out there. So the landlord might have the BlackJoker and RedJoker as a rocket. If I play the 2s, and the landlord has the rocket, they can play it to beat. But rockets can beat anything. But if they don\'t have the rocket, then the 2s would be a strong play. \n\nBut the landlord has 18 cards. It\'s possible they have the BlackJoker, but not sure. If I play the 2s, and the landlord has the rocket, they can play it. But if they don\'t, then the 2s are a strong play. However, the teammate has 15 cards left. If I play the 2s, maybe the landlord can\'t respond, and the next turn is my teammate\'s. They might have the BlackJoker to play as a rocket, but they are a teammate. Hmm, but the teammate\'s cards are not visible. \n\nAlternatively, playing the Queens is a safer option. It\'s a higher pair than Jacks but not the highest. If the landlord can\'t beat it, then the play is mine, and the teammate can follow up. But if the landlord has a higher pair, like Kings or Aces, they can play. But looking at the card counts, Aces have 3 left. So maybe the landlord has some Aces. But the landlord has 18 cards, so they might have some high cards. \n\nBut the main point is that I need to beat the current play. The pair of Queens is a valid and sufficient move. If I 